\subsection{Probe Architecture Sensitivity}
\label{sec:linear_probe}

To rule out that our findings depend on MLP probe capacity ($N/\text{params} \approx 0.06$, overparameterized), we replicate the per-layer analysis with a linear probe ($4{,}096 \to 7$, $N/\text{params} \approx 2.2$).
The inverted-U pattern reproduces: trained image\_mean peaks at L14 ($R^2 = 0.565$), while untrained declines monotonically from L0 ($R^2 = 0.517$).
The trained--untrained gap is larger under the linear probe (L14: $\Delta = +0.324$) than the MLP probe (L8: $\Delta = +0.015$); VLA training thus reorganizes representations to make action information more linearly decodable.
Untrained deep layers collapse entirely under the linear probe (L25: $R^2 = -0.298$); MLP nonlinearity partially rescues these degraded representations.
For last\_preaction tokens, the linear probe also corroborates the inverted-U (peak L13, $R^2 = 0.596$), with the same peak layer as the MLP probe and a trained-vs-untrained gap of $\Delta = 0.103$ ($p < 0.001$).
These results indicate that our qualitative findings reflect representational structure rather than probe capacity artifacts (Figure~\ref{fig:linear_vs_mlp}).

